{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/940396-story-games","id":940396,"title":"Story-games?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-07-30T19:43:44-07:00","id":940396,"title":"Story-games?","content":"I re-posted this on the WoD forum, since i'm interested in both community's opinions and not everyone reads both.<br /> <br /> So, it's something I'm thinking about recently, namely modern story-games and their mechanics. Admittedly, I haven't played any so far, but read the CofD corebook and the Requiem 2e book and getting interested in the Dresden Files rpg, because I love the novels. However as I read about the core assumptions of the FATE system, I've been getting the picture that there is an emphasis on mechanics that allows the player to directly influence the story and I have a problem with that, because it somewhat breaks the immersion and suspension of disbelief for me. It feels a little too game-y, if I'm not interacting with the story through my character's actions, but directly. I'm totally into collaborative world-building between the ST and players, I think it adds tremendously to the game and the player's interest in the story and their characters, but altering the story and events out of character with a game-mechnaic during actual play feels somewhat off to me.<br /> <br /> Now, I think I understand the appeal of that type of gaming, it could be a wonderful experience of collective story-telling, but somehow it feels to me a little different thing than what I picture as role-playing. Not worse just different.<br /> <br /> I think it's relevant for WoD, because the new WW guys are coming from nordic larp, so I get the vibe that the new edition of the WoD games might be moving toward the story-game style from old-school rpg and these type of games seems to be the hype of nowadays.<br /> <br /> What do you think? Are you enjoying story-games and mechanics? How do you feel and think about them? Is there something about them that I missed? How these mechanics playing out in practice in CofD games?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-30T19:42:14-07:00","id":940395,"title":"","content":"I re-posted this on the CofD forum, since i'm interested in both community's opinions and not everyone reads both.<br /> <br /> So, it's something I'm thinking about recently, namely modern story-games and their mechanics. Admittedly, I haven't played any so far, but read the CofD corebook and the Requiem 2e book and getting interested in the Dresden Files rpg, because I love the novels. However as I read about the core assumptions of the FATE system, I've been getting the picture that there is an emphasis on mechanics that allows the player to directly influence the story and I have a problem with that, because it somewhat breaks the immersion and suspension of disbelief for me. It feels a little too game-y, if I'm not interacting with the story through my character's actions, but directly. I'm totally into collaborative world-building between the ST and players, I think it adds tremendously to the game and the player's interest in the story and their characters, but altering the story and events out of character with a game-mechnaic during actual play feels somewhat off to me.<br /> <br /> Now, I think I understand the appeal of that type of gaming, it could be a wonderful experience of collective story-telling, but somehow it feels to me a little different thing than what I picture as role-playing. Not worse just different.<br /> <br /> I think it's relevant for WoD, because the new WW guys are coming from nordic larp, so I get the vibe that the new edition of the WoD games might be moving toward the story-game style from old-school rpg and these type of games seems to be the hype of nowadays.<br /> <br /> What do you think? Are you enjoying story-games and mechanics? How do you feel and think about them? Is there something about them that I missed?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-30T19:53:14-07:00","id":940400,"title":"","content":"Having moved towards a lot of storygames (FATE, Dramasystem, even GUMSHOE somewhat counts), I honestly can't imagine ever going back. Roleplaying is already collaborative storytelling; making that more implicit in the rules is only a good thing in my eyes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-31T12:40:26-07:00","id":940755,"title":"","content":"Interesting. Different tastes I guess, for me it seems metagaming. Also it depends on what someone wants from an rpg? If the focus is the 'drama' (another buzzword this times) and weaving the most dramatic story together, I think it is desirable. My focus is firstly exploration and seeing the world through my character's eyes (this includes playing my character as immersively as I could, though there is a limit) and secondly the sole drama factor.<br /> <br /> If in the middle of the play I could directly influence the events through a game-mechanic instead of saying &quot;my character doing this and that, let's see what happens&quot;, that's reminding me I'm playing a game and breaks the notion that my character is part of a living, breathing world. Mind, it's entirely a different thing for me, than integrating the player's ideas into the game and setting and things like WoD backgrounds. In non-storygames, my interface is the character and the framework is the rules. In a storygame I feel the interface is the story-rules as much as the character.<br /> <br /> It might be that I see this much more extremely than it really is, as I said I don't have any practical experiences so far and I'm not against story-games, I'm absolutely willing to try them if I have the opportunity, and even I won't like them, lots of people do and it's perfectly fine. I'm mostly interested in what others see in these games in counter to classic-style rpgsand how different people handle the metagaming feel I get from these games.<br /> <br /> <br /> PS: it seems like the two topic get merged. Are the moderators did it for some reason, or it happened because of the same title? I'm realy interested in both the WoD and CofD community's opinions since I think there might be a difference.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-31T13:58:24-07:00","id":940834,"title":"","content":"You assume metagaming is intrinsically a problem. Many games do not treat it as such. Old School D&amp;D basically required you to Metagame in subterranian Fantasy Vietnam of it all. As long as its not cheating it really shouldn't be seen as a problem.<br /> <br /> Honestly I've found story gaming helps to drive characters into tragic situations because you can still reward the player for doing things the Character would not do on theirown. By separating rewards purely from an in universe level players feel more comfortable doing things that their character totally would do that are bad for them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-31T14:44:57-07:00","id":940860,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You assume metagaming is intrinsically a problem. Many games do not treat it as such. Old School D&amp;D basically required you to Metagame in subterranian Fantasy Vietnam of it all. As long as its not cheating it really shouldn't be seen as a problem. </div> </div> </div>I don't see metagaming itself as a 'problem'. I generally just don't like it in the sense that making IC choices from the standpoint (and because) of OOC knowledge and mechanics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Honestly I've found story gaming helps to drive characters into tragic situations because you can still reward the player for doing things the Character would not do on theirown. By separating rewards purely from an in universe level players feel more comfortable doing things that their character totally would do that are bad for them. </div> </div> </div>I see, this absolutely could have be a merit to encourage players doing those things. However, again, different styles and expectations. If I'm taking every time the most dramatic way of doing things and especially if I'm systematically choosing ways for my character with the goal of failing spectacularly instead of succeeding, or taking unnecessary risks because of OOC mechanics and rewards, then it's breaking verisimilitude for me, because it encourages certain ways of behavior which might be in contrast with my character's personality and encourages, again OOC thinking instead of 'living' the character and interacting the setting through it in a way that is in-line with it. On the other side, NOT doing things which my character'd totaly do, but it'd be bad for him, because I know it'd be bad for him is also metagaming.<br /> <br /> I guess, in the end, I'm more comfortable with believable character actions (logical or illogical), than with choosing actions with the goal of 'making the most dramatic scenario', or 'collect beats'. I'm uncomfortable for example in DnD with the notion of killing an NPC, because that rewards more XP than negotiating with him (and really, it shouldn't).<br /> <br /> I'm not against drama and dramatic scenarios (actually, I like them very much! *), but I prefer them to be the result of IC choices and story circumstances, not the main goal of playing, because it shifts the whole focus of the game from personalizing 'living' characters, exploring the setting and trying to achieve personal goals, to collaboratively writing a screen-play with the goal of making it spectacular. It's not wrong, actually, I think I'd enjoy it time-to-time and the more I'm thinking about it, the more I think it suits better one-shots, or short chronicles with that special focus, but it's still a bit contradictory with what I'm expecting from 'default' role-playing.<br /> <br /> *Although if drama on itself is too much in focus, I feel it has the potential do devalue it. Like too much action and/or suffering raises the tolerance level, taking the edge of it. That, and I certainly have a limit for drama for drama's sake, but I'm more of an explorer and problem-solver type than pure actor type. That's one of my fears regarding the new edition of WoD from the nordic LARP girls and guys (however, I'm admiring their enthusiasm and currently hoping for the best), namely too much emphasis and focus on 'soap opera' instead of setting, history, politics, intrigue etc. which I enjoy in WoD.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 07-31-2016, 06:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-31T15:44:43-07:00","id":940885,"title":"","content":"Lets look at it from what I call the &quot;Toreador Problem&quot;. The most rational action a character can come to as a Toreador is to flee all art. To hate Beauty and be surounded by the most plain of things. This is something they can readily and easily test ICly so the most reasonable thing ICLY to do is be completely at odds with how the setting presents things. This isn't any more metagaming than determining you can frenzy or sunlight burns. Its an easily observed scientific fact in setting.<br /> <br /> Now instead of being &quot;Sticked&quot; into beauty lets swap things around. Lets assume there's a &quot;carrot&quot; you get some sort of benny each time you see something new and beautiful as toreador to represent the pleasure they receive from observation. Now the Player can act &quot;optimally&quot; while the character can act on impulse.<br /> <br /> Impulse is a powerful bit of versimitude for me, the lack of it throws me out of any sense of &quot;reality&quot; for the setting when everyone is making super optimal choices. There's a thread in the Vampire forum that drifted onto whether or not vampires should still be racist. The argument against it is that Vampire society is so SO darwinian that any suboptimal choice would lead to death so everyone always makes perfect optimal choices. That completely shatters any sense of versimitude.. I mean if that's the case why are there bigots in reality? Shouldn't its suboptimal fashion have lead to destruction over time?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-31T16:44:11-07:00","id":940928,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Lets look at it from what I call the &quot;Toreador Problem&quot;. The most rational action a character can come to as a Toreador is to flee all art. To hate Beauty and be surrounded by the most plain of things. This is something they can readily and easily test ICly so the most reasonable thing ICLY to do is be completely at odds with how the setting presents things. This isn't any more metagaming than determining you can frenzy or sunlight burns. Its an easily observed scientific fact in setting. </div> </div> </div>Please don't mistake logic and reason with what is 'logical' from the character's standpoint. We all do illogical things all the time, because it feels good, and that's the contrast between your example of sunlight and beauty, the later doesn't hurt, on the contrary, I always interpreted their reverie as something very sensuable, the closest thing they could feel to being in love, or to the embrace. Is it dangerous? Yes, but also blood-bonding with a loved one, or consuming blood from people who is high. Moreover, you can't determine what'd trigger the reverie and most people don't want to cloister themselves for the sake of safety, on the contrary actually. Moreover, the Torreador's drawn to beauty is more of the result of their mortal lives's experiences and/or the inner culture of the clan I think. If the Torreador in the example won't go outside and cut herself from beautious things, he would be miserably lonely, won't experience the positive sides of reverie and become left out from the social life. both of these are enough IC reasons to not do it for me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now instead of being &quot;Sticked&quot; into beauty lets swap things around. Lets assume there's a &quot;carrot&quot; you get some sort of benny each time you see something new and beautiful as toreador to represent the pleasure they receive from observation. Now the Player can act &quot;optimally&quot; while the character can act on impulse. </div> </div> </div>I see your point with the carrot thing (and certainly seeing the trend in games to do more carrot and less stick) and really don't see a problem here, however, honestly, these awards are already incorporated into receiving XP for me, notably XP for roleplaying the character appropriately. If you don't go outside, don't get into trouble and experience new things, well, no XP for you, and honestly, if you just want to sit home with you char, why you play? I never really understood the burden of getting the characters into the action, because as I wrote earlier, I'm naturally an explorer type so 'doing the adventure' is the basic premise for me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Impulse is a powerful bit of versimitude for me, the lack of it throws me out of any sense of &quot;reality&quot; for the setting when everyone is making super optimal choices. There's a thread in the Vampire forum that drifted onto whether or not vampires should still be racist. The argument against it is that Vampire society is so SO darwinian that any suboptimal choice would lead to death so everyone always makes perfect optimal choices. That completely shatters any sense of versimitude.. I mean if that's the case why are there bigots in reality? Shouldn't its suboptimal fashion have lead to destruction over time? </div> </div> </div>Always making the super-optimal choices regardless of the character's personality/goals/cicumstances is just as metagaming as always taking unnecessary risks and always doing suboptimal things for OOC rewards. I think both approach and style are gameable and certain players will game it anyway.<br /> <br /> My biggest concern I think is not with giving awards for active roleplaying, but for the mechanics that allow the player to actively alter the events outside of the actions of the character, so as I wrote giving the player an OOC interface. It's just strengthen the feel of gaming for me and I'd have a harder time to immerse in the game's world. I have similar feelings toward too much minigames for certain parts, like social interaction, or assign too much rules to personal connections.<br /> <br /> Note that I didn't have any strong objections against the CofD mechanics, I found most of them okay, but I read discussions about the Dresden rpg where posters said it is very metagame-y. i don't want to form an opinion before I had the chance to read the whole of it, but I thought the topic is worth discussing it.<br /> <br /> PS: Oh, and I'm mostly agreeing with you regarding of the other topic, I understand why people want to shred the garbage of real life in their power fantasy make-believe games, but I also on the side of making realistic people in the settings and people, are frankly, f*cked-up and I don't see why they'd be less so in the WoD, which is even darker than ours. Just because here and now a lot of people (myself included) want a liberal, egalitarian society and social justice is one of the biggest buzzwords in the western culture, it doesn't mean that the prince of the city who flogged slaves to death on his plantation would see this way. Or that the secluded elders of Assamites would embrace (in any meaning) every culture and race on earth. And, for me this whole thing gives another layer and depth to the war of ages and anarchs and whatnot, which is good. It gives the setting depth and history, but history isn't pretty.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 07-31-2016, 07:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-31T17:16:07-07:00","id":940947,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/940396-story-games?p=940928#post940928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Please don't mistake logic and reason with what is 'logical' from the character's standpoint. We all do illogical things all the time, because it feels good, and that's the contrast between your example of sunlight and beauty, the later doesn't hurt, on the contrary, I always interpreted their reverie as something very sensuable, the closest thing they could feel to being in love, or to the embrace. Is it dangerous? Yes, but also blood-bonding with a loved one, or consuming blood from people who is high. Moreover, you can't determine what'd trigger the reverie and most people don't want to cloister themselves for the sake of safety, on the contrary actually. Moreover, the Torreador's drawn to beauty is more of the result of their mortal lives's experiences and/or the inner culture of the clan I think. If the Torreador in the example won't go outside and cut herself from beautious things, he would be miserably lonely, won't experience the positive sides of reverie and become left out from the social life. both of these are enough IC reasons to not do it for me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except the game doesn't support a mechanic of &quot;your character feels miserable&quot; and a player can easily say &quot;Well an alcaholic character feels miserable too its still in his best interests to not go near the bottle&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I see your point with the carrot thing (and certainly seeing the trend in games to do more carrot and less stick) and really don't see a problem here, however, honestly, these awards are already incorporated into receiving XP for me, notably XP for roleplaying the character appropriately. If you don't go outside, don't get into trouble and experience new things, well, no XP for you, and honestly, if you just want to sit home with you char, why you play? I never really understood the burden of getting the characters into the action, because as I wrote earlier, I'm naturally an explorer type so 'doing the adventure' is the basic premise for me. </div> </div> </div>There's a difference between &quot;I avoid all adventure&quot; and &quot;I avoid impulse&quot;. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Always making the super-optimal choices regardless of the character's personality/goals/cicumstances is just as metagaming as always taking unnecessary risks and always doing suboptimal things for OOC awards. I think both approaches and styles are gameable and certain players will game it anyway. </div> </div> </div>Impulse isn't about personality or goals. It just is. I point to the toreador/alcaholic problem the game rewards you for not giving in. It helps you survive attacks. You will get xp for it, ti even makes perfect sense ICLY that someone knowing they lose control would avoid things. How do I properly simulate &quot;the character feels so good they don't care in this moment?&quot; That's what the carrot for the player si for.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My biggest concern I think is not with giving awards for active roleplaying, but for the mechanics that allow the player to actively alter the events outside of the actions of the character, so as I wrote giving the player an OOC interface. It's just strengthen the feel of gaming for me and I'd have a harder time to immerse in the game's world. I have similar feelings toward too much minigames for certain parts, like social interaction, or assign too much rules to personal connections. </div> </div> </div>All Players have an OOC interface. its called &quot;Game mechanics&quot; that some games are better at obfuscating the system is a thing some players need to get involved. Others have issues with &quot;lets just rp it out&quot; for social situations where player x can't try to every play a charismatic character because.. they are shy. In the end is arbitrary personal preference and how much or how little is again personal preference.<br /> <br /> To me I like being able to play a character who is smarter or more social than me not just someone who's stronger and can hit people harder.<br /> <br /> [quote[Note that I didn't have any strong objections against the CofD mechanics, I found most of them okay, but I read discussions about the Dresden rpg where posters said it is very metagame-y. i don't want to form an opinion before I had the chance to read the whole of it, but I thought the topic is worth discussing it.[/QUOTE]<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/114903/Fate-Core-System?hot60=0&amp;src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=0_0_44284_0_0\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/...=0_0_44284_0_0</a> Fate is pay what you want read it for yourself. Rather than let certain words come at you with certain expectations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-31T18:01:54-07:00","id":940969,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/940396-story-games?p=940947#post940947\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Except the game doesn't support a mechanic of &quot;your character feels miserable&quot; and a player can easily say &quot;Well an alcaholic character feels miserable too its still in his best interests to not go near the bottle&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Except the game has rules for addiction, willpower, and acting on nature and demeanor.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's a difference between &quot;I avoid all adventure&quot; and &quot;I avoid impulse&quot;. </div> </div> </div>Sure. But if you avoid impulse, you maybe won't get WP as often and you'll be left out from the socializing, so it has IC and OC consequences potentially. And you can't avoid all impulse, like a beautiful landscape, or person, or the whirls of smoke drifting above the bar's tables.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Impulse isn't about personality or goals. It just is. I point to the toreador/alcaholic problem the game rewards you for not giving in. It helps you survive attacks. You will get xp for it, ti even makes perfect sense ICLY that someone knowing they lose control would avoid things. How do I properly simulate &quot;the character feels so good they don't care in this moment?&quot; That's what the carrot for the player si for. </div> </div> </div>Sure, but will the Ventrue gain xp, or beats for drinking from their favored type, or the Brujahd for losing control? Okay, that's a moot question, under the recent CofD rules they will, of course. I think it's just a personal preference of giving rewards for failing or not. I understand the reasoning behind it (more carrot, more player enjoyment, the sense of accomplishing something, etc.), just don't personally think it necessary. The strict experience part is already incorporated in the xp for participating and roleplaying for me and rewarding failing could led just as many problems as harshly penalizing it. I could bring an example from one of my other interests, sword fighting (HEMA), where the community struggles with the after-blow rule. It means that after receiving a hit, I could still hit you within one tempo (one movement more or less). It's goal is to encourage proper defense after the attack from the attacker's side, but it often resulted in neglecting it on the defender's side, because if I could null your hit with my hit, why defend? I just wrote it down to highlight that any approach could lead to &quot;gaming the rules&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> All Players have an OOC interface. its called &quot;Game mechanics&quot; that some games are better at obfuscating the system is a thing some players need to get involved. Others have issues with &quot;lets just rp it out&quot; for social situations where player x can't try to every play a charismatic character because.. they are shy. In the end is arbitrary personal preference and how much or how little is again personal preference. </div> </div> </div>I disagree. As I wrote earlier, I prefer to interact with the story and the game through my character (and it's action's results and consequences), as an interface, which operates in the framework of the game's system as we IRL operating in the system of physics, etc. The story mechanics are giving me another layer of direct interaction with the story which is strictly OOC. It's absolutely different than having rules for non-physical actions, I have zero problems with those (just don't necessarily think that they need whole minigames, but again, personal tastes).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To me I like being able to play a character who is smarter or more social than me not just someone who's stronger and can hit people harder. </div> </div> </div>Me too. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/114903/Fate-Core-System?hot60=0&amp;src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=0_0_44284_0_0\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/...=0_0_44284_0_0</a> Fate is pay what you want read it for yourself. Rather than let certain words come at you with certain expectations. </div> </div> </div>I will, but ah, time issues and too much on the reading list already! Anyway thanks for the link I'll try to digest it and form an opinion after that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-31T20:52:03-07:00","id":941017,"title":"","content":"I think of it as the opposite. A Toreador who avoids art is guilty of bad RP, so they need to be punished for breaking an explicit promise that they made by taking that clan. It is the same thing when people take flaws. If you are addicted to alcohol, then you should seek alcohol under normal circumstances and I will penalize you for trying to avoid it unless it is necessary for the story. Characters get rewarded for their flaws during character creation, and I see no reason to reward them again during the game (I personally hate flaws that drive stories because it does not reward anyone else in the group).<br /> <br /> As an ST, you interact with the players through the story and the NPCs. If a PC is an alcoholic, then they should have alcoholic friends that lead him into temptation. If a character is a Toreador, their clan mates should demand that they go with them to participate in the culture of their city. Otherwise, why did you let them take that flaw or select that clan?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-31T21:54:31-07:00","id":941034,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/940396-story-games?p=941017#post941017\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think of it as the opposite. A Toreador who avoids art is guilty of bad RP, so they need to be punished for breaking an explicit promise that they made by taking that clan. It is the same thing when people take flaws. If you are addicted to alcohol, then you should seek alcohol under normal circumstances and I will penalize you for trying to avoid it unless it is necessary for the story. Characters get rewarded for their flaws during character creation, and I see no reason to reward them again during the game (I personally hate flaws that drive stories because it does not reward anyone else in the group).<br /> <br /> As an ST, you interact with the players through the story and the NPCs. If a PC is an alcoholic, then they should have alcoholic friends that lead him into temptation. If a character is a Toreador, their clan mates should demand that they go with them to participate in the culture of their city. Otherwise, why did you let them take that flaw or select that clan?</div> </div> </div> </div>While I'm mostly agreeing with you, please forgive me, but I think you're a little harsh here. I certainly see some merit in mechanics that encourage players to play out their characters (flaws in this case) more and I understand that some players need more encouraging and rewarding, but I think it could lead to problems (but again, the opposite approach too). I agree, that flaws and their mechanics handle that, in addition to rewarding xp, but I see the merits of the CofD system (positive reinforcing and all that) albeit I found them not necessary <i>for me. </i>I don't have any real problem with them by and large, I just feel more comfortably, with the WoD mechanics and playing out the char as much as I could is just natural desire, but some of this feeling is certainly just habitual. Albeit yes, it's problematic for me to gain xp for, for example being alcoholic and indulging in it. A point of wp? Sure, I can rationalize it. But xp? What I learned from it? Your character has flaws, has weaknesses, play it accordingly, I don't feel that further awarding is necessary.<br /> <br /> Regarding the Toreador, I think there is merit in playing against the stereotypes, but really, how could you avoid to seeing any beautiful and remarkable things barring don't go out from the 4x4 gray concrete room? But i agree (I think) that I found in-game, in-character consequences and NPC reactions a better way to communicate with the players than a mechanic.<br /> <br /> I guess, my main points are:<br /> <br /> - Rewarding certain behaviors is a double-edged sword.<br /> - I prefer role-playing games as role-playing instead of collaborative story writing on the level of the actual story through an OOC mechanic. For me the collaborative storytelling is realized in playing one of the protagonists and influence the world through it's actions. If I can say &quot;no, the enemy don't get to escape, his car's tire are deflated!&quot; and spend a point to make it so, it's just too gamey for me. My goal as a player is to 'living' that character, experiencing a good story and 'writing' that story through the character's participation, not writing it directly.<br /> - Thus strong narrative mechanics on the player's part breaks my immersion and my desire to explore the world through the character (BUT I absolutely love collaborative world-building, like actual setting-building and elaborating backgrounds and backstory <i>out of</i> the game and in downtimes). As a ST, like players who ask questions and get ideas thus giving me ideas and participating in the making progress.<br /> - Drama is great, I enjoy good drama tremendously when it's happening naturally, but playing with the primary goal of making drama feels excessive. There is a point for me when it just goes to soap-opera field.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 08-01-2016, 12:57 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-31T22:28:38-07:00","id":941040,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/940396-story-games?p=941017#post941017\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think of it as the opposite. A Toreador who avoids art is guilty of bad RP, so they need to be punished for breaking an explicit promise that they made by taking that clan. It is the same thing when people take flaws. If you are addicted to alcohol, then you should seek alcohol under normal circumstances and I will penalize you for trying to avoid it unless it is necessary for the story. Characters get rewarded for their flaws during character creation, and I see no reason to reward them again during the game (I personally hate flaws that drive stories because it does not reward anyone else in the group).<br /> <br /> As an ST, you interact with the players through the story and the NPCs. If a PC is an alcoholic, then they should have alcoholic friends that lead him into temptation. If a character is a Toreador, their clan mates should demand that they go with them to participate in the culture of their city. Otherwise, why did you let them take that flaw or select that clan?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'm not a fan of Flaws at character Creation vs multifaceted aspects ala Fate or Bonds/afflictions in Nobilis/Chuubos but Toreador is an example we both know. In either of those games you might take &quot;Toreador&quot; as stat and when you say want to know about art, or be a social butterfly in the elysium you could use it as such.. when it gets you in trouble you get benefits.<br /> <br /> Its a different style of game play. And I don't find &quot;Punish the players&quot; really only leads to anything but a dead game. If a player wants to have a character who is a recovering alcaholic I don't see why they shouldn't have the option. And really I think the carrot works much better than the stick to get people to roleplay.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-31T22:52:23-07:00","id":941048,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/940396-story-games?p=941034#post941034\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">- Rewarding certain behaviors is a double-edged sword.</div> </div> </div> </div>You asked how these things have worked for particular people.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> - I prefer role-playing games as role-playing instead of collaborative story writing on the level of the actual story through an OOC mechanic. For me the collaborative storytelling is realized in playing one of the protagonists and influence the world through it's actions. If I can say &quot;no, the enemy don't get to escape, his car's tire are deflated!&quot; and spend a point to make it so, it's just too gamey for me. My goal as a player is to 'living' that character, experiencing a good story and 'writing' that story through the character's participation, not writing it directly. </div> </div> </div>Lets use a different example. Say I want to play a Chessmaster. a Lelouch Vi Britania, a Batman you know a guy who's a master planner. But I'm not a master planner. I'm not a transcendent Ancient bloodgod. I'm just an average person with average intelligence.<br /> <br /> So I have a &quot;power&quot; on the sheet that represent My character is good at planning.<br /> <br /> So bad guy is driving away I say &quot;I have this power on my sheet that represents i&quot;m a super planner I messed with his car before we got into the confrontation so his car is broken!&quot;<br /> <br /> Or my character is a precog. The mechanics of this is rather than having the St constantly giving me flashes of what's going to happen in 2 minutes. I get spend something and retcon an action that just happened because &quot;I knew it was going to happen&quot; in this case my precog broke the car on the way in because character is psychic.<br /> <br /> I feel abit like you are missing a point its like complaining &quot;In vampire I roll dice and characters just die. I don't understand how that works&quot; On a purely metamechancial level that's true but it also ignores the fact that rolling those dice can mean something in setting like stabbing someone or setting them on fire.<br /> <br /> Declarations are perhaps the closest thing to what you are talking about... but they represent &quot;I'm really fucking smart&quot; your character does research about a monster they fought last night and then you do a declare action &quot;they are weak to Blessed Salt&quot; this isn't a &quot;my player just spends a bennie and suddenly monster is weak to salt&quot; its &quot;My character is smart enough to discover a weakness!&quot; its just represented in a different fashion than a standard roll.<br /> <br /> Now these still might not be your cup of tea. Maybe you do prefer the St doesnt' make stuff up on the fly. I personally like proactive players in a fairly sandboxy setup vs people who want to follow a plot.. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-01T00:24:20-07:00","id":941081,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/940396-story-games?p=941048#post941048\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You asked how these things have worked for particular people.</div> </div> </div> </div>I did and your comments and opinion are appreciated. It shed lights to some of my concerns and even it is a clear difference in our preferences, I value you taking time to write all of that down, thanks!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Lets use a different example. Say I want to play a Chessmaster. a Lelouch Vi Britania, a Batman you know a guy who's a master planner. But I'm not a master planner. I'm not a transcendent Ancient bloodgod. I'm just an average person with average intelligence.<br /> <br /> So I have a &quot;power&quot; on the sheet that represent My character is good at planning.<br /> <br /> So bad guy is driving away I say &quot;I have this power on my sheet that represents i&quot;m a super planner I messed with his car before we got into the confrontation so his car is broken!&quot;<br /> <br /> Or my character is a precog. The mechanics of this is rather than having the St constantly giving me flashes of what's going to happen in 2 minutes. I get spend something and retcon an action that just happened because &quot;I knew it was going to happen&quot; in this case my precog broke the car on the way in because character is psychic. </div> </div> </div>I'd prefer other ways to do it, maybe slipping some extra information to the player, or making suggestions, or just asking for an intelligence or perception roll. I'd try not saying what he can do, but giving him information and options. It's not really that different from what you're saying, but I'd prefer not doing it retroactively, baring time-magic involved. I feel it detracts from the natural flow of the game and diminishes the action's consequences.<br /> <br /> In the end both approaches encourage creativity, so neither is &quot;bad&quot;, I just feel the latter more natural thus helping the immersion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I feel abit like you are missing a point its like complaining &quot;In vampire I roll dice and characters just die. I don't understand how that works&quot; On a purely metamechancial level that's true but it also ignores the fact that rolling those dice can mean something in setting like stabbing someone or setting them on fire. </div> </div> </div>No, I'm perfectly avare that every rules are abstractivisms, thanks. But I think abstractivism is not a yes/no thing, it's a scale and rules that represent OOC narrative-altering are a bit too abstract for me, but it's personal perception and reading how other people perceive these things are interesting to me even if I don't agree with them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Declarations are perhaps the closest thing to what you are talking about... but they represent &quot;I'm really fucking smart&quot; your character does research about a monster they fought last night and then you do a declare action &quot;they are weak to Blessed Salt&quot; this isn't a &quot;my player just spends a bennie and suddenly monster is weak to salt&quot; its &quot;My character is smart enough to discover a weakness!&quot; its just represented in a different fashion than a standard roll. </div> </div> </div>Why not just gave him information after the research? I'm not against creativity and players adding to my already made-up concepts, or altering them. If it sounds fun, run with it! But doing it in the middle of action, saying &quot;You know what, my research sayed this monster is vulnerable to salt. Oh look, i just happened to have a pocketfull of salt from the yesterday's ghost hunting...&quot; it's just a bit too convenient.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now these still might not be your cup of tea. Maybe you do prefer the St doesnt' make stuff up on the fly. I personally like proactive players in a fairly sandboxy setup vs people who want to follow a plot.. </div> </div> </div>I apologize, but you seem misunderstandig it if you see it as a sandbox/railroading question. I prefer consistency setting-wise, this doesn't exclude making up things on the fly and i love and encourage it when players are willing to add personal pieces to the story and setting. I also don't think that the form of interaction with the narrative and setting (strictly through the character vs. direct narrative rules) is any indication to the proactiveness of the players.<br /> <br /> It eventually boils down to different people expects and enjoys different kinds of play and interaction with the game and have different priorities. I have certain preferences, but seeing how others are see particular concepts and prefer different styles, even arguing about them is always interesting! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-01T08:10:11-07:00","id":941198,"title":"","content":"I always like the 'all roads lead to the same point' style of being an ST. If I want player's to find a clue then, regardless of the red herrings they follw, they will find the clue. In one of my games, I used a botch of a driving roll to have the PC plow into a tractor trailer that was carrying the girls that they were supposed to rescue three sessions beforehand. Dealing with the events of that botch took twenty sessions and the players never knew that they had missed the first story hook.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-01T16:38:30-07:00","id":941500,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/940396-story-games?p=941081#post941081\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I'd prefer other ways to do it, maybe slipping some extra information to the player, or making suggestions, or just asking for an intelligence or perception roll. I'd try not saying what he can do, but giving him information and options. It's not really that different from what you're saying, but I'd prefer not doing it retroactively, baring time-magic involved. I feel it detracts from the natural flow of the game and diminishes the action's consequences.<br /> <br /> In the end both approaches encourage creativity, so neither is &quot;bad&quot;, I just feel the latter more natural thus helping the immersion.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> I don't think its particularly natural. Nothing has killed my sense of immersion more than attempts to make thing as close to the character as player because well there's going to be clear points of failure. If everything is abstact and systemy my brain puts that in the background. If everything basically relys on the player(which first leads me to player isn't playing character but player is playing self) and then suddenly we are doing dice for combat or picking locks or whatever nothing throws me out of it liek that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why not just gave him information after the research? I'm not against creativity and players adding to my already made-up concepts, or altering them. If it sounds fun, run with it! But doing it in the middle of action, saying &quot;You know what, my research sayed this monster is vulnerable to salt. Oh look, i just happened to have a pocketfull of salt from the yesterday's ghost hunting...&quot; it's just a bit too convenient. </div> </div> </div><br /> Its no more or less &quot;convenient&quot; than any other mechanic for it. Abstractly &quot;I spend resources to damage this target&quot; It could be just as readily skinned as &quot;we perform this 12 hour ritual to make this Knife a bane to Monster&quot;<br /> <br /> You're also coming at this from a place where &quot;beat the monster&quot; is the only success/failure reward point. Doing it in a &quot;too convenient&quot; method for the players/GM has its own issues.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I apologize, but you seem misunderstandig it if you see it as a sandbox/railroading question. I prefer consistency setting-wise, this doesn't exclude making up things on the fly and i love and encourage it when players are willing to add personal pieces to the story and setting. I also don't think that the form of interaction with the narrative and setting (strictly through the character vs. direct narrative rules) is any indication to the proactiveness of the players. </div> </div> </div>There's a WIDE range between railroading and Sandboxes. I am saying in my personal experience the more say a player has in the situation the more proactive the characters can be. That doesn't mean a more structured set up leads to a railroad. Just that certain people thrive in greater structured setups.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-01T22:55:18-07:00","id":941714,"title":"","content":"One problem that I have commonly encountered in story games is over identification of players with their characters. I have seen friendships end when a player's character was killed after attempting a stupid stunt for a better story for their character (oh, my racist character is going to go into a gang stronghold shouting racial insults, how many experience points do I get?). A flaw that does not reward a player after character creation is something to overcome, a potential story hook, which can add flavor to a campaign. A flaw that grants rewards after character creation is nothing more than an excuse to grandstand and make problems for the rest of the group.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-01T23:04:47-07:00","id":941718,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/940396-story-games?p=941714#post941714\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One problem that I have commonly encountered in story games is over identification of players with their characters. I have seen friendships end when a player's character was killed after attempting a stupid stunt for a better story for their character (oh, my racist character is going to go into a gang stronghold shouting racial insults, how many experience points do I get?).</div> </div> </div> </div>That has nothing to do with story games asside from &quot;That's how the character died&quot; I mean I've straight up seen friendships destroyed because of a bad dice roll, or someone having an off night. Deciding to go pvp because &quot;that's what my character would do&quot; someone who'd get pissed over their character dying will do so unless your system is &quot;you can't die period&quot; and Storytelling games are more likely to have that sort of mechanic<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A flaw that does not reward a player after character creation is something to overcome, a potential story hook, which can add flavor to a campaign. A flaw that grants rewards after character creation is nothing more than an excuse to grandstand and make problems for the rest of the group. </div> </div> </div><br /> No its not, its a valuable source of plot and gives an St a a hand rather than putting everything on their back.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}